WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.029
A merchant opens their dashboard.

2
00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:11.311
The backend asks 100 servers for one piece each, all at once, and waits for every answer.

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.054
Each server usually answers in 5 to 15 milliseconds.

4
00:00:15.054 --> 00:00:19.428
But one call in a hundred hits a pause, and takes a full second.

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:21.073
99 servers are done.

6
00:00:21.073 --> 00:00:24.959
The page still waits for the last one, a whole second.

7
00:00:25.167 --> 00:00:28.145
One server is slow one time in a hundred.

8
00:00:28.145 --> 00:00:31.196
The page is fast only if all 100 are fast.

9
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:37.378
That's 0.99 multiplied by itself 100 times, about 0.37.

10
00:00:37.378 --> 00:00:42.379
So 63 page loads in 100 take a second or more.

11
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:45.082
So stop waiting for the unlucky server.

12
00:00:45.082 --> 00:00:49.727
Keep each piece of data on a second server too, a copy called a replica.

13
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:50.839
Run it again.

14
00:00:50.839 --> 00:00:53.766
At 15 milliseconds, one call is still out.

15
00:00:53.766 --> 00:00:57.249
So the backend sends the same call to the replica.

16
00:00:57.433 --> 00:01:00.411
The backend takes whichever answer comes first.

17
00:01:00.411 --> 00:01:04.529
Here it's the replica's, and the page is done at 23 milliseconds.

18
00:01:04.733 --> 00:01:07.508
That second call is a hedged request.

19
00:01:07.508 --> 00:01:10.507
Only late calls get one, about 1 in 100.

20
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:17.699
Google tested it on a database, BigTable, reading 1,000 keys spread over 100 servers.

21
00:01:17.699 --> 00:01:27.332
Hedging after 10 milliseconds cut the slowest read in a thousand from 1,800 milliseconds to 74, for 2% more requests.

22
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:28.896
It needs two things.

23
00:01:28.896 --> 00:01:31.961
The replica must be slow for its own reasons.

24
00:01:31.961 --> 00:01:35.980
If one bad deploy slows both copies, the hedge is slow too.

25
00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:38.739
And the call must be safe to run twice.

26
00:01:38.739 --> 00:01:39.399
A read is.

27
00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:44.016
A card payment needs a key that the issuer checks, so it charges once.

28
00:01:44.233 --> 00:01:48.595
When a page waits for 100 servers, the slowest one sets its time.

29
00:01:48.595 --> 00:01:52.353
A hedge asks a second server and keeps the first answer.
